{
  "source": "course-assets/creative-thinking/creative-thinking.mp4",
  "duration": 188.1386875,
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 6.76,
      "text": "Think back to the last time someone told you good idea. It feels great to hear, but why did they actually say it?"
    },
    {
      "start": 7.66,
      "end": 11.88,
      "text": "Most likely you saw something they missed or you found a more effective way to move forward."
    },
    {
      "start": 12.5,
      "end": 14.74,
      "text": "It's the ability to see a problem differently."
    },
    {
      "start": 15.68,
      "end": 19.0,
      "text": "Question the obvious approach and find an angle other people missed."
    },
    {
      "start": 20.04,
      "end": 28.18,
      "text": "We often assume this kind of thinking belongs strictly to artists, writers and musicians, but problem solving requires the exact same skill."
    },
    {
      "start": 28.8,
      "end": 34.5,
      "text": "Take Steve Jobs. He wasn't a painter or a novelist, but he was highly effective at finding new angles."
    },
    {
      "start": 34.98,
      "end": 43.86,
      "text": "He looked at early, complicated desktop computers and saw a possibility others overlooked. They could be simplified, made personal and easy to use."
    },
    {
      "start": 44.62,
      "end": 50.86,
      "text": "True creativity is noticing the gaps and possibilities that everyone else walks right past, regardless of your profession."
    },
    {
      "start": 51.9,
      "end": 55.28,
      "text": "Creativity isn't a job title or an innate personality trait."
    },
    {
      "start": 55.28,
      "end": 60.32,
      "text": "It is a habit of thinking that kicks in whenever a standard answer simply isn't enough."
    },
    {
      "start": 61.3,
      "end": 64.82,
      "text": "This graphic breaks down how that habit looks across four distinct fields."
    },
    {
      "start": 65.66,
      "end": 66.36,
      "text": "Look at the top left."
    },
    {
      "start": 67.22,
      "end": 73.6,
      "text": "A lawyer examines the exact same case file, laws and facts as everyone else, but finds a completely new strategy."
    },
    {
      "start": 74.54,
      "end": 81.76,
      "text": "Next to that an entrepreneur surveys the market, spots a consumer need that everyone else overlooked and builds a new way to meet it."
    },
    {
      "start": 82.42,
      "end": 88.84,
      "text": "Down on the bottom left, when standard blueprints hit a dead end, an engineer designs a solution that sidesteps the roadblock."
    },
    {
      "start": 89.7,
      "end": 95.54,
      "text": "And finally, a doctor can look at a common set of symptoms and deduce a diagnosis that others completely missed."
    },
    {
      "start": 96.58,
      "end": 102.84,
      "text": "Across all these fields, the difference between an average result and an exceptional one comes down to this habit of thinking."
    },
    {
      "start": 103.76,
      "end": 109.32,
      "text": "Today we have artificial intelligence. You can type a prompt and get a polished standard answer in seconds,"
    },
    {
      "start": 109.85,
      "end": 111.7,
      "text": "but getting an answer is only the beginning."
    },
    {
      "start": 112.54,
      "end": 118.02,
      "text": "Your advantage is noticing what's missing, connecting ideas from different places and choosing a better direction."
    },
    {
      "start": 118.66,
      "end": 124.8,
      "text": "That human advantage is a magic gift. You can actively build and improve it through specific daily practice."
    },
    {
      "start": 125.64,
      "end": 129.62,
      "text": "This guide outlines four concrete habits you can use to generate better solutions."
    },
    {
      "start": 130.58,
      "end": 134.54,
      "text": "The first habit, shown on the top left, is to generate before you judge."
    },
    {
      "start": 135.26,
      "end": 139.0,
      "text": "Write down several ideas, including the bad ones, before you decide what works."
    },
    {
      "start": 139.32,
      "end": 142.56,
      "text": "The most obvious standard ideas usually arrive first."
    },
    {
      "start": 143.56,
      "end": 150.24,
      "text": "Next, on the top right, ask what if. Take one core rule or assumption about your problem and change it."
    },
    {
      "start": 150.64,
      "end": 153.04,
      "text": "See what happens if the exact opposite were true."
    },
    {
      "start": 153.76,
      "end": 156.46,
      "text": "Third, on the bottom left, connect unrelated things."
    },
    {
      "start": 157.08,
      "end": 163.44,
      "text": "Look for a pattern, feature, or approach from a completely different industry and try applying it to your current roadblock."
    },
    {
      "start": 164.08,
      "end": 165.98,
      "text": "Finally, step away, then return."
    },
    {
      "start": 167.0,
      "end": 170.86,
      "text": "Focus intensely on the problem and then go take a walk or switch tasks."
    },
    {
      "start": 171.5,
      "end": 175.3,
      "text": "New mental connections often form in the background when your attention moves elsewhere."
    },
    {
      "start": 176.16,
      "end": 179.88,
      "text": "These four habits widen your options. Then judgment picks the one that fits."
    },
    {
      "start": 180.6,
      "end": 183.44,
      "text": "AI widens the options. You still pick the better angle."
    }
  ],
